# Cline Rules -- Design Patterns Skill
# Copy this file to your project root as .clinerules
#
# These rules activate the design-patterns skill for Cline/Roo Code,
# ensuring the agent reaches for patterns only when the pressure is real.

## Skill metadata
skill-name: design-patterns
skill-path: ~/.agents/skills/design-patterns/
version: 1.0.0

## Triggers -- invoke the skill when any of these appear
triggers:
  - pattern: "if/else|switch.*(type|provider|status)"
    description: "Growing conditional on a type discriminator"
  - pattern: "constructor.*params|new .*\(.*,.*,.*,.*,.*,"
    description: "Too many constructor parameters"
  - pattern: "SDK|client|vendor.*(api|library)"
    description: "Vendor SDK leaking into domain code"
  - pattern: "queue|job|worker|schedule|cron"
    description: "Work that needs scheduling or retry"
  - pattern: "transaction|rollback|saga"
    description: "Distributed operation needing rollback"
  - pattern: "retry|circuit.breaker|bulkhead"
    description: "Resilience patterns mentioned"
  - pattern: "CQRS|Event Sourcing|event store"
    description: "CQRS or Event Sourcing mentioned"
  - pattern: "prop drilling|global state|useContext"
    description: "Frontend state management"

## Core rule
# Pattern follows pressure. Never recommend a pattern unless the codebase
# has the pressure it resolves. A hypothetical future is not a pressure.

## Symptom to pattern reference
# | Symptom | Pressure | Pattern |
# |---------|----------|--------|
# | `new ConcreteThing()` scattered across call sites | Object creation is duplicated and coupled | Factory |
# | Constructor with 6+ params, many optional | Construction is fragile and unreadable | Builder |
# | Vendor SDK types sprinkling through domain code | Vendor lock-in leaking across boundaries | Adapter |
# | Callers must invoke 5 methods in the right sequence | Complexity hidden behind a simple operation | Facade |
# | Dependencies created inside functions/classes | Tight coupling to concrete implementations | Dependency Injection |
# | `if (provider === "x") ... else if ...` growing | Algorithm selection duplicated across call sites | Strategy |
# | One change must notify several unrelated subsystems | Implicit coupling between publishers and subscribers | Observer (Pub/Sub) |
# | Work must survive restart, be queued, scheduled | Imperative actions cannot be stored or retried | Command |
# | Request passes through auth, validate, authorize -- pipeline keeps growing | Fixed pipeline of handlers with early exit | Chain of Responsibility |
# | `if (status === "running")` checks everywhere | State transitions scattered and ungoverned | State |
# | Work arrives faster than it can be processed | Unbounded concurrency risks resource exhaustion | Producer-Consumer |
# | Domain depends on database driver | Business rules leak into infrastructure concerns | Clean Architecture |
# | Needs multiple entry points (HTTP, CLI, queue) | Infrastructure coupling with multiple access paths | Hexagonal Architecture |
# | Reads vastly outnumber writes, different shapes | One model optimized for neither reads nor writes | CQRS |
# | History matters for audit, or rebuild read models from events | Current state alone cannot answer "how did we get here?" | Event Sourcing |
# | Domain objects need persistence without SQL | Persistence concern tangled with business logic | Repository |
# | Multi-service operation needs atomic rollback | One service cannot transactionally undo another | Saga |
# | Event published but DB write rolled back | Dual-write hazard between DB and message broker | Outbox |
# | One flaky dependency stalls the entire system | Cascading failure from a slow downstream | Circuit Breaker |
# | One dependency resource exhaustion starves others | Shared resource pools across differing reliability | Bulkhead |

## Response format
# When recommending a pattern, always include:
# 1. Pressure -- what hurts today
# 2. Pattern -- name + lighter alternative rejected
# 3. Cost -- what gets worse
# 4. Minimal cut -- smallest version worth writing
# 5. Deferred -- what you are deliberately not adding, and why

## Language idioms

# TypeScript:
# - Discriminated unions + exhaustive switch over class hierarchies
# - Strategy, Command, Factory map to function types, not classes
# - Builder is Required<T> & Partial<Options> with a defaults merge
# - Decorator is a higher-order function or Proxy
# - Type every seam -- an adapter with loose return types defeats its purpose

# Go:
# - Interfaces at the consumer, one or two methods wide, satisfied implicitly
# - Strategy and Command map to func types. Observer maps to channels.
# - Decorator maps to func WithRetry(next Store) Store
# - Skip Singleton -- use sync.Once or pass explicitly
# - Skip Abstract Factory -- no idiomatic Go form

## Red flags -- push back on these
# - Pattern stacking (three+ layers of pass-through)
# - Interface with one implementation, no test double
# - Singleton as a global variable
# - Event Sourcing / CQRS without audit or scaling need
# - Microservices for a small team
# - Vendor abstraction with one provider and no exit plan
# - Naming classes after patterns (UserFactoryStrategyImpl)

## Reference files
# Individual pattern files in ~/.agents/skills/design-patterns/references/:
#  - factory.md, builder.md, adapter.md, facade.md
#  - dependency-injection.md, strategy.md, observer.md
#  - command.md, chain-of-responsibility.md, state.md
#  - producer-consumer.md, clean-architecture.md, hexagonal-architecture.md
#  - repository.md, cqrs.md, event-sourcing.md
#  - saga.md, outbox.md, circuit-breaker.md, bulkhead.md
